  "version": 1,
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Workers × Hours × Days: The Work Budget",
    "description": "Use worker-hours-days as one work budget for equal-efficiency labour.",
    "teachingApproach": "Introduce one worker-hour as a unit of effort. For equal-efficiency workers, total work is proportional to workers × hours per day × days. Build from same-work problems to finding any missing factor, then add changed work amount and output quantity. Use cancellation-first arithmetic and finish with the chapter-style symbolic x/y pattern.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 30,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 15, Chain Rule. Approved scope: Teach the work-budget model for equal-efficiency workers: work is proportional to workers × hours per day × days. Cover finding days, workers, hours per day, output quantity, changed amount of work, half-time/double-work patterns, and symbolic forms. Use cancellation-friendly mental arithmetic. Distinguish 'same work' from 'more work' so learners know when work amount must enter the chain."
  },
